\f1\fs22 Johnny reached out to Shakti Civil Engineering, who promptly designed a set-up according to his requirement, that is, \'93Make that sucker spin.\'94 The engineers, visibly delighted to take a break from their usual work designing \'93defensive\'94 staplers and land-mine toasters for the Peacemaking Division, produced a design in a matter of days. Johnny reviewed the drawings and technical specs for roughly 13.6 seconds and, having concluded that Eloise\'92s engineers couldn\'92t be trusted to design a swizzle stick, launched into designing and building his own apparatus from parts he pulled from masonry drills, washing machines, vacuum cleaners, electric can openers and his grandmother\'92s antique waffle iron. The result, completed in a fever dream at four in the morning after binging on tequila and a bucket of fried chicken, might generously be called a \'93vertical lathe,\'94 though calling it that was rather like calling nitroglycerin \'93a liquid\'94\'97technically accurate but missing several crucial details about instability and sudden detonation.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Shakti\'92s engineers took one look at Johnny\'92s new design and went over his head. Approval came down to build their design while the Johnny\'92s contraption, and every loose bolt and pipe cleaner, was compacted and incinerated. The remains were scattered in fifteen different garbage dumps.\
Before week\'92s end, the Johnny stood in the test bunker inspecting the new apparatus, was genuinely stunned. He hated crediting anyone but himself with competence, but had to admire the machine, as baroque and over-engineered as a prize-winning wedding cake. To cover his awe, he sneered at the Shakti engineers with, \'93You\'92re sure this won\'92t blow the whole place up?\'94 Because, as ever, sniping was so much easier than anything resembling gratitude.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The technical staff were as jittery as ever, painstakingly mounting the disc, tightening the jaws, and adjusting the chuck. Johnny, in a surprisingly good mood, skipped the usual pranks. In the control room, he wedged his pear-shaped form into the groaning director\'92s chair, donned his headset, winced as it snagged his last hair, and fussed at his scalp before giving up. He luxuriated briefly in the power to command this many plebeians, then started flicking switches as if divining life into the lights and screens.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \'93Initiate,\'94 he intoned, graveyard-shift DJ smooth. \'93Thirty revs per minute.\'94\
\'93Thirty revs per minute,\'94 echoed an anonymous tech; Johnny didn\'92t care who.\
All eyes fixed on the monitors. In the bunker, those within view nervously peered from behind thick, laminated glass. The disc turned, leathery and dull, about as fast as a whirling dervish. A hush fell. Techs, transfixed, began to sway, hands and arms lifted in an involuntary, vaguely ritual motion that crept around the room like a syncopated contagion. In the control room, chairs began spinning as everyone synchronised with the disc.\
Everyone slipped deeper into trance; everyone except Johnny, who was far too interested in the dim parade of his own reflections dancing in the control room glass walls.\
He didn\'92t notice what was happening until his next order\'97\'93Sixty revs per minute\'94\'97met only silence. Annoyed, he looked up to find his team twirling their chairs, fingers gesturing in trancelike unison. Snapping from late-night DJ to breakfast show host, he barked, \'93Sixty revs per minute, now!\'94\
The techs snapped out of it, recalibrated themselves, and the disc\'92s spin accelerated. Behind the glass, the syncopated dance resumed.\
\'93No change in energy signatures, sir,\'94 a tech reported, as ingratiatingly as possible.\
Johnny smiled. Always satisfying to have sycophants properly aligned. \'93Double it,\'94 he said, feeling potent.\
\'93One twenty RPM, sir.\'94\
\'93No change in energy signatures, sir.\'94\
Johnny let the satisfaction fill him. \'93Now\'97accelerate at sixty revolutions per minute squared.\'94\
\'93Yes, sir.\'94\
Within a few minutes, two things happened: the dancing bunker techs tripped over themselves and hit the floor, and the ground beneath the facility began to shake, softly at first, then violently.\
\'93This\'92ll cause an earthquake... Sir!\'94 someone yelled.\
\'93We\'92re getting energy fluctuations!\'94\
The monitors showed wild energy blooming off the disc\'97rainbows and sheens refracting off its surface, bouncing madly around the chamber.\
\'93Sir, we need to shut it down!\'94\
Johnny hesitated, unwilling to abandon his hero posture as the world trembled around him. Some self-destructive drive told him this, finally, was real heroism, if only because it was so obviously dangerous.\
For a moment, Johnny dissociated\'97drifting to a warm memory: his first car. It had, of course, been forbidden by his parents (\'93a death trap and a status symbol,\'94 they\'92d called it), so he bought it anyway; with personal money, which was to say, an allowance from the family account that outstripped the budgets of most local councils.\
Back in the present, Johnny was certain the disc and its machine behaved almost exactly like his beloved car on a cold morning\'97its engine, a bombastic marvel, would stutter and protest at low revs, but would, in his hands, become a purring threat the closer he pushed it to redline.\
\'93It\'92s just hitting destabilising resonance,\'94 Johnny said. \'93It\'92ll stabilise at higher revs. Thirty thousand revs per minute.\'94\
\'93Sir?\'94\
\'93Red-line it.\'94\
By a form of blind luck usually reserved for the terminally privileged, the tremors subsided as machine and disc settled into a kind of monstrous harmony as the rev rate increased towards red-line. Johnny\'92s minions marvelled; sir\'92s recklessness was working. Johnny chortled.\
The disc never reached thirty thousand RPM. Long before then, it erupted, spraying furious blue lightning. Energy tore through the bunker, shredding machines and fixtures, scorching the walls. The metal-polycarbonate observation window held, etched now with galactic chaos. Lethal radiation, fiercer than any occupational standard ever conceived, lanced through the glass. The techs behind it were vaporised\'97what remained of them stewing on the floor like dirty laundry in a bouillabaisse of their own steaming liquids.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The eruption etched a pattern across the observation window: a sort of pan-dimensional calling card for whatever built the disc. It wasn\'92t so much a symbol as a vindictive geometry project\'97fractal, recursive, and deeply smug about it.  \
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Concentric spirals radiated outward, each one curling not just horizontally, but in directions best left to mathematicians and hyperactive string theorists. At small scales, the spirals broke into smaller whorls\'97a near-perfect mimicry of everything from a snail shell to a galaxy, to that pattern you get on your laptop screen after whacking it with a frying pan. Lines looped and overlapped, as if doodled by a cosmic hand trembling with both caffeine and fundamental anxiety, weaving lattices that shifted when you looked away, as if shy of being fully understood.\
Edge-on, the whole looked almost like a galaxy cluster seen through glass smeared with quantum uncertainty, but from the side resolved into branching filaments and fizzing knots\'97like the cosmic microwave background, if the universe had briefly turned to doodling on the back of the equations. Take a step sideways, and it morphed into river deltas, neural webs, or the flickering afterglow of the universe\'92s very first bright idea. The longer you observed, the more the shapes seemed to unfold, hinting at extra dimensions, or at least the gnawing sense that dimensions beyond three were out there gossiping about your haircut.\
It was, Johnny realised, almost like looking back through time itself. Not just to the Big Bang, but to the moment before the universe decided which way was \'93up\'94, back when lines and fractals and maths first got their bright idea to become reality. In that dizzy moment, Johnny felt as if the universe itself had pressed its thumbprint in the condensation of the observation window, if only to remind him who was boss.\
Or, failing that, it was at least the universe\'92s best attempt at forging parking permits for anything above the tenth dimension.\
Johnny, as the trembling subsided, allowed himself a sneer. At least they\'92d learned something. Perhaps finally, it was time to stop getting kinetic on the disc\'92s arse.
